# L §5.3 Entry into force (round 16): "for each row of the version, the later of the version's `known` and the start of
# the row's own time (P-6): its own span, lifespan or instant where it states one, else the version's `governs` ... So a
# version with no `governs`, all of whose rows carry their own time, still enters into force row by row"; L §4.3 the
# `wording D for [...]` pin. v2 (known 2026-01-10, no governs): its x row governs from 2026-03, so it enters into force
# on 2026-03-01; its y row from 2026-01, so on 2026-01-10. At the law date 2026-04-01: x - y = 2 - 3 = -1 (q1); in the
# wording of 2026-02-01, x is v1's 1 and y is 3: -2 (q2); in that of 2026-03-01 both rows: -1 (q3).
=== m.tally
model t
  tally 2
  on 2026-12-31
  start 2026-01-01
  end 2026-12-31

source s "s"

input x: int
input y: int

def xy: int at reader
  rule base = x - y

version v1
  cite s
  governs 2025..
  known 2025-01-01
  x = 1
  y = 1

version v2
  cite s
  known 2026-01-10
  replace x = 2 governs 2026-03..
  replace y = 3 governs 2026-01..

ask q1: xy law 2026-04-01
ask q2: xy as if {law 2026-04-01, wording 2026-02-01 for [x, y]}
ask q3: xy as if {law 2026-04-01, wording 2026-03-01 for [x, y]}
=== expect
q1: -1
  law date 2026-04-01, cut 2026-12-31
q2: -2
  pinned: law 2026-04-01
  pinned: wording 2026-02-01 for [x, y]
  cut 2026-12-31
q3: -1
  pinned: law 2026-04-01
  pinned: wording 2026-03-01 for [x, y]
  cut 2026-12-31
